/**
 * @file load-env.js
 * @brief The environment bootstrap of the CloudAvid File Portal: reads the project-root .env file into process.env at boot — preferring the dotenv package, silently falling back to a hand-rolled parser — so that server.js's PORT and lib/files-service.js's FILES_ROOT are real before a single request arrives.
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section le_overview 📖 Overview
 *
 * @subsection le_eli5 🧒 ELI5 Summary
 *
 * Imagine the portal is a **restaurant** that opens every morning.
 * Before the first customer walks in, someone has to read the **day's
 * instruction sheet** taped to the back door: "we open at port 3000, the
 * food is stored in the warehouse behind the building". That instruction
 * sheet is the `.env` file, and this file is the **person who reads it out
 * loud** first thing in the morning.
 *
 * The reader is also very polite. There are two ways the sheet can be read:
 * the fancy way (a professional reader the restaurant hired — the `dotenv`
 * package) and, if that employee didn't show up, a simple way (the manager
 * reads it by hand — the small parser in this file). And the reader never
 * shouts over someone who already knows the answer: if the boss already
 * wrote the opening time on a sticky note (a real environment variable set
 * by the operating system or Docker), the reader does **not** replace it
 * with what the sheet says. The sheet is only for things nobody has
 * mentioned yet.
 *
 * If the sheet is missing or torn, the reader doesn't panic and doesn't
 * close the restaurant — they just quietly start work with whatever they
 * remember, and the doorman (`server.js`) picks a default door number (3000)
 * while the kitchen keeps asking "where is the warehouse?" (which later
 * becomes the `503 FILES_ROOT_MISSING` answer from `lib/files-service.js`).
 *
 * @subsection le_responsibilities Core Responsibilities
 *
 * - **One-Shot Bootstrap:** `loadEnv(projectRoot)` is called exactly once,
 *   synchronously, at the top level of `server.js`, before `lib/files-service.js`
 *   is even required. It populates `process.env` for the whole Node process.
 * - **Preferred Path — dotenv:** Tries `require("dotenv").config({ path: envPath })`
 *   first. dotenv is a declared dependency in `package.json`
 *   (`"dotenv": "^16.4.7"`), so in a normal install this is the path taken.
 * - **Fallback Path — Hand-Rolled Parser:** If dotenv cannot be loaded, a
 *   self-contained `parseEnvText()` performs a minimal KEY=VALUE parse so the
 *   server still boots with a broken/absent `node_modules`.
 * - **Never-Override Policy:** Both paths only write a variable when it is
 *   currently `null`, `undefined` or `""` — real environment variables
 *   (Docker, systemd, CI) always win over `.env`.
 * - **Never-Throw Contract:** `loadEnv()` catches every failure and degrades
 *   to a `console.warn` at worst. Boot never crashes because of a bad `.env`.
 * - **Windows/Linux Robustness:** The parser splits on `/\r?\n/`, so `.env`
 *   files edited on Windows (CRLF) and Linux (LF) both parse correctly.
 *
 * @subsection le_arch_role 🧭 Role in the Overall Architecture
 *
 * This file is the **first rung of the back-end's dependency ladder**. The
 * boot order in `server.js` is load-bearing:
 *
 * - `server.js` computes `PROJECT_ROOT = __dirname` and calls
 *   `loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)` at module top level.
 * - Only then does `server.js` read `process.env.PORT || 3000` and require
 *   `./lib/files-service`.
 * - `lib/files-service.js` reads `process.env.FILES_ROOT` lazily inside every
 *   request (`resolveConfiguredRoot()`), which is why it is also safe.
 *
 * Upward, this module is consumed by exactly one file: `server.js`
 * (`const { loadEnv } = require("./lib/load-env");`). Downward, it depends on
 * two Node built-ins (`fs`, `path`), the optional `dotenv` package, and the
 * `.env` file itself (documented by `.env.example`). It has **no** knowledge
 * of HTTP, the download tree, or the frontend.
 *
 * @note What This File Does NOT Do:
 * - It does **not** validate values. `PORT=banana` is loaded verbatim; the
 *   consequences surface later in `server.js` (`listen` failure) or nowhere.
 *   `FILES_ROOT` validation (exists? is a directory? has categories?) belongs
 *   entirely to `lib/files-service.js`.
 * - It does **not** support dotenv's richer syntax: no `export KEY=VALUE`
 *   prefix, no multi-line values, no `${VAR}` interpolation, no inline
 *   comment stripping (`PORT=3000 # comment` keeps ` # comment` in the value
 *   on the fallback path). Those are dotenv-only conveniences.
 * - It does **not** log success. A successful load is completely silent;
 *   only a failed fallback read produces output (`console.warn`).
 * - It does **not** watch or reload `.env`. It runs once at module-load time;
 *   editing `.env` requires a server restart.
 * - It does **not** protect itself over HTTP. `server.js`'s security
 *   blocklist (`pathname === "/.env" || pathname.startsWith("/lib/") || ...`
 *   → 403) is what stops anyone downloading this module or the real `.env`
 *   over the network — a job that lives in `server.js`, not here.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section le_arch 🏗️ Architectural Flow
 *
 * The diagram below shows the complete decision tree that runs once at boot.
 * Note the two-level fallback: dotenv → manual parser → give up quietly.
 *
 * @dot
 * digraph loadenv_flow {
 *   rankdir=TB;
 *   node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#9CA3AF"];
 *   edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9, color="#6B7280"];
 *
 *   start   [label="npm start → node server.js\n(server.js top level)", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   call    [label="loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)\nPROJECT_ROOT = __dirname", fillcolor="#DBEAFE"];
 *   join    [label="path.join(projectRoot, \".env\")\n→ envPath", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *
 *   trydotenv [label="try: require(\"dotenv\").config({ path: envPath })", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   dotenvok  [label="dotenv installed and config ran", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *   dotfallok [label="dotenv missing OR config threw\n(bare catch → fall through)", fillcolor="#FCE7F3"];
 *
 *   exists  [label="fs.existsSync(envPath)", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   read    [label="fs.readFileSync(envPath, \"utf8\")\n→ parseEnvText(text)", fillcolor="#FEF3C7"];
 *   noop    [label="No .env — silent no-op\n(boot continues with defaults)", fillcolor="#FCE7F3"];
 *   warn    [label="console.warn(\"Could not read .env:\", err.message)\nnever re-throws", fillcolor="#FCE7F3"];
 *
 *   env     [label="process.env populated\n(PORT, FILES_ROOT, …)", fillcolor="#FFEDD5"];
 *
 *   port    [label="server.js:\nconst PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *   fsvc    [label="lib/files-service.js:\nresolveConfiguredRoot() reads\nprocess.env.FILES_ROOT per request", fillcolor="#D1FAE5"];
 *
 *   start -> call -> join -> trydotenv;
 *   trydotenv -> dotenvok [label="success → return"];
 *   trydotenv -> dotfallok [label="require/config throws"];
 *   dotenvok -> env;
 *   dotfallok -> exists;
 *   exists -> read [label="true"];
 *   exists -> noop [label="false"];
 *   read -> warn [label="read/parse throws"];
 *   read -> env [label="success"];
 *   noop -> env;
 *   warn -> env;
 *
 *   env -> port;
 *   env -> fsvc;
 * }
 * @enddot
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section le_sequence 🔄 Execution Sequence
 *
 * The runtime interaction between `server.js`, this module, the optional
 * `dotenv` package, the fallback parser, and the two consumers of the
 * populated environment.
 *
 * @startuml
 * skinparam backgroundColor transparent
 * skinparam participantPadding 10
 * skinparam boxPadding 6
 *
 * actor       "Operator\n(npm start)"              as Op
 * participant "server.js\n(top-level bootstrap)"  as Srv
 * participant "lib/load-env.js\nloadEnv()"        as LE
 * participant "dotenv package\n(optional)"        as Dot
 * participant "parseEnvText()\n(fallback parser)" as PE
 * participant "Node fs"                           as FSys
 * participant "process.env"                       as Env
 * participant "lib/files-service.js\n(lazy consumer)" as FSvc
 *
 * Op -> Srv : node server.js
 * Srv -> LE : loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)
 * activate LE
 * LE -> Dot : require("dotenv").config({ path: "<root>/.env" })
 *
 * alt dotenv present and config succeeds
 *   Dot -> Env : write keys that are not already set\n(PORT, FILES_ROOT)
 *   LE --> Srv : return
 * else require throws (dotenv not installed) or config throws
 *   LE -> FSys : fs.existsSync(envPath)
 *   alt .env exists
 *     FSys --> LE : true
 *     LE -> FSys : fs.readFileSync(envPath, "utf8")
 *     LE -> PE : parseEnvText(text)
 *     activate PE
 *     loop every non-empty, non-"#" line containing "="
 *       PE -> Env : process.env[key] = value\n(only if unset or "")
 *     end
 *     deactivate PE
 *   else .env missing
 *     LE --> LE : silent no-op
 *   else readFileSync throws (permissions, encoding)
 *     LE -> Srv : console.warn("Could not read .env:", err.message)
 *   end
 * end
 * deactivate LE
 *
 * Srv -> Env : process.env.PORT || 3000
 * Srv -> FSvc : require("./lib/files-service")
 * note right of FSvc : required AFTER loadEnv() —\nordering is load-bearing
 *
 * == later, once per API request ==
 * FSvc -> Env : process.env.FILES_ROOT
 * FSvc --> Srv : categories / listings / download targets
 * @enduml
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section le_blocks 🧩 Block-by-Block Deep Dive
 *
 * The file is 42 lines long and every one of them is documented below, in
 * source order: the header comment, the two built-in requires, the fallback
 * parser, the orchestrating loader, and the export statement.
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection sub_le_header Block: File Header Comment
 *
 * - **🎯 Purpose:** The opening comment states the file's entire contract in
 *   one sentence — what it loads (`.env`), from where (the project root),
 *   and how (prefer `dotenv`, fall back to a small parser). It is the
 *   documentation-canary for the dual-path design: if anyone ever makes
 *   dotenv a hard requirement, this sentence becomes a lie and must change.
 * - **🧒 ELI5:** This is the name tag on the reader's uniform: "Hi, I read
 *   the instruction sheet. I have two ways of reading it."
 * - **⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step):**
 *   1. It is a plain `/* ... *\/` block comment; JavaScript discards it at
 *      parse time. It has zero runtime effect.
 *   2. Its value is for humans and for this documentation set: the house
 *      pattern in this repo is that every `lib/` module opens with such a
 *      one-line mission statement (see `lib/files-service.js`), which keeps
 *      `git grep` hit lists readable.
 *   3. Note that it is **not** a Doxygen comment (`/**`), so Doxygen does not
 *      attach it as file documentation — this page is that documentation.
 * - **📥 Inputs:** None.
 * - **📤 Outputs:** None (pure comment).
 * - **🔗 Project Dependencies:**
 *     - Depends on: nothing.
 *     - Consumed by: human readers, documentation passes (`dox/main/server.dox`
 *       paraphrases this exact sentence when describing the bootstrap).
 *     - @see server.js
 *     - @see lib/files-service.js
 * - **⚠️ Breaking Change Warning:** Functionally nothing — but this sentence
 *   is quoted/paraphrased in `dox/main/server.dox`'s "Environment Bootstrap"
 *   section. Editing the wording here creates a documentation inconsistency;
 *   changing the *meaning* (e.g. dropping the fallback) invalidates that
 *   section and the `@dot` flow diagram in this file.
 *
