 * @snippet lib/load-env.js le_snip_header
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection sub_le_requires Block: Built-In Requires (fs, path)
 *
 * - **🎯 Purpose:** Imports exactly the two Node built-ins the module needs:
 *   `fs` for checking and reading `.env` on the fallback path, and `path`
 *   for joining the project root with the filename in an OS-portable way.
 * - **🧒 ELI5:** The reader's two tools: a magnifying glass to check the
 *   sheet exists (`fs`) and a map to find the back door (`path`).
 * - **⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step):**
 *   1. `const fs = require("fs");` — the synchronous callback-style fs API
 *      (not `fs/promises`): `loadEnv` itself is deliberately synchronous, so
 *      it can run at module top level before any async machinery exists.
 *      (Contrast with `lib/files-service.js`, which uses `fs/promises` for
 *      request-time work — a deliberate split: sync for boot, async for
 *      requests.)
 *   2. `const path = require("path");` — used once, in `loadEnv`, via
 *      `path.join(projectRoot, ".env")`. `join` (not manual string
 *      concatenation) is what makes `loadEnv` behave correctly on Windows
 *      backslash paths and POSIX forward-slash paths alike.
 *   3. Neither is used by `parseEnvText`, which is pure string processing —
 *      a small sign of good separation: the parser is testable without any
 *      filesystem.
 * - **📥 Inputs:** None.
 * - **📤 Outputs:** Two module-scope bindings, `fs` and `path`, used only by
 *   `loadEnv`.
 * - **🔗 Project Dependencies:**
 *     - Depends on: Node.js built-ins only — no third-party packages here,
 *       matching the "dependency-free small utility" house style.
 *     - Consumed by: `loadEnv` (the only function that touches disk).
 *     - @see lib/files-service.js — uses `fs/promises` + `fs` + `path` for
 *       the request-time side of the same application.
 *     - @see server.js — same "built-ins first" require style.
 * - **⚠️ Breaking Change Warning:** Removing or misspelling either require
 *   produces a `ReferenceError` at require time inside `server.js`'s boot
 *   chain — the entire server fails to start, not just the `.env` loading.
 *   Because `server.js` requires this module before `lib/files-service.js`,
 *   a crash here also means the domain layer never loads.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/load-env.js le_snip_requires
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection sub_le_parseenvtext Block: parseEnvText(text) — The Fallback Parser
 *
 * - **🎯 Purpose:** A miniature `.env` parser used only when `dotenv` cannot
 *   be required. It converts raw file text into `process.env` writes with
 *   just enough syntax support to handle the keys this project actually
 *   uses (`PORT`, `FILES_ROOT` — see `.env.example`), including quoted
 *   Windows paths like `FILES_ROOT="D:\CloudAvid\Downloads"`.
 * - **🧒 ELI5:** When the professional reader is absent, the manager reads
 *   the sheet line by line: skip the blank lines and the lines that start
 *   with `#` (those are doodles, not instructions), find the `=` sign, and
 *   read the left side as the label and the right side as the value —
 *   politely removing quotation marks if the value wears a coat. And the
 *   golden rule: never say anything someone has already said.
 * - **⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step):**
 *   1. **Split:** `text.split(/\r?\n/)` breaks the file into lines while
 *      swallowing the `\r` of Windows CRLF endings — essential because this
 *      repository is edited on Windows (`server.js` is CRLF) and deployed on
 *      Linux.
 *   2. **Trim:** each raw line is `trim()`-ed, so leading/trailing spaces
 *      around keys, values and separators are tolerated.
 *   3. **Skip:** `if (!line || line.startsWith("#")) continue;` — empty lines
 *      and full-line comments are ignored. (Inline trailing comments are
 *      *not* stripped; see the Note below.)
 *   4. **Separate:** `const eq = line.indexOf("=");` finds the **first**
 *      `=`. `if (eq <= 0) continue;` rejects both lines with no `=` (`eq ===
 *      -1`) and lines where `=` is the first character (`eq === 0`, i.e. an
 *      empty key). A value that itself contains `=` survives intact, because
 *      only the first occurrence splits.
 *   5. **Key/Value:** `key = line.slice(0, eq).trim()`,
 *      `value = line.slice(eq + 1).trim()`.
 *   6. **Unquote:** if the value both starts *and* ends with `"` (or both
 *      with `'`), the outer quote pair is sliced off
 *      (`value = value.slice(1, -1)`). The length-≥-2 requirement is
 *      implicitly guaranteed: a string that starts and ends with the same
 *      character has at least that length. Only symmetric pairs are
 *      stripped; `"value'` is left untouched.
 *   7. **Non-Override Write:** the guard
 *      `if (process.env[key] == null || process.env[key] === "")` uses loose
 *      `== null` (catches both `undefined` and `null`) plus the empty-string
 *      case, then writes `process.env[key] = value`. A variable set by the
 *      OS, Docker or CI always wins over the `.env` value — the same
 *      non-override semantics dotenv uses by default.
 *   8. **No return value:** the function communicates exclusively through
 *      the side effect on `process.env` — it returns `undefined`.
 * - **📥 Inputs:** `text` — the full UTF-8 contents of `.env` as a string.
 * - **📤 Outputs:** Mutates `process.env` (adds keys only when unset/empty);
 *   returns `undefined`. Throws nothing for malformed lines (they are
 *   silently skipped), so one bad line never blocks the others.
 * - **🔗 Project Dependencies:**
 *     - Depends on: nothing but the global `process` object — no `fs`, no
 *       `path`, no dotenv.
 *     - Consumed by: `loadEnv` only. It is deliberately **not exported**
 *       (see the exports block), keeping the module's public surface at
 *       exactly one function.
 *     - @see .env.example — the two keys it must understand (`PORT`,
 *       `FILES_ROOT`), including the quoted-Windows-path case.
 *     - @see lib/files-service.js — `resolveConfiguredRoot()` later consumes
 *       `process.env.FILES_ROOT`, whatever parser wrote it.
 * - **⚠️ Breaking Change Warning:** This function is the *fallback of the
 *   fallback*, which makes it the easiest place to introduce silent
 *   misconfiguration:
 *   - Removing the `== null || === ""` guard makes `.env` override real
 *     environment variables — in Docker/systemd deployments that export
 *     `PORT`/`FILES_ROOT`, the containerised values would be silently
 *     clobbered by a stale `.env` committed to the image.
 *   - Removing the `startsWith("#")` skip turns comment lines into
 *     garbage keys (they fail the `eq <= 0` test only when they contain no
 *     `=` — a comment containing `=` would create a bogus variable).
 *   - Loosening `eq <= 0` to `eq === -1` would accept `=value` lines and
 *     create empty-string keys in `process.env`.
 *   - Changing the regex to `split("\n")` breaks Windows-authored `.env`
 *     files: every value would gain a trailing `\r`, and
 *     `FILES_ROOT=D:\CloudAvid\Downloads\r` resolves to a directory that
 *     does not exist → every listing request 503s with
 *     `FILES_ROOT_NOT_FOUND`.
 *   - Adding inline-comment stripping changes values that legitimately
 *     contain ` #` (e.g. passwords in quoted strings) — do not "fix" this
 *     without checking dotenv's exact semantics.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/load-env.js le_snip_parseenvtext
 *
 * @note The parser handles the syntax this project needs, not the full
 * dotenv grammar. Unsupported (dotenv-only) constructs on the fallback path:
 * `export KEY=VALUE` (key becomes `export KEY`), multi-line values,
 * `${VAR}` interpolation, and inline trailing comments. If a deployment
 * relies on any of these, ensure `node_modules/dotenv` is actually installed
 * (it is declared in `package.json`, so a plain `npm ci` guarantees it).
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection sub_le_loadenv Block: loadEnv(projectRoot) — The Orchestrator
 *
 * - **🎯 Purpose:** The module's entire public API. Loads `<projectRoot>/.env`
 *   into `process.env` once, preferring dotenv, degrading gracefully, and
 *   never throwing.
 * - **🧒 ELI5:** The morning routine: find the instruction sheet on the back
 *   door, call the professional reader if they showed up today, otherwise
 *   read it yourself, and if the sheet is missing or torn, just shrug and
 *   start the day anyway.
 * - **⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step):**
 *   1. `const envPath = path.join(projectRoot, ".env");` — resolves the
 *      absolute `.env` path from the caller-supplied root. `server.js` passes
 *      `PROJECT_ROOT` (= `__dirname`), so in production this is
 *      `<repo>/.env`, exactly where `.env.example` says to create it.
 *   2. **Preferred path:** inside a `try`, `require("dotenv").config({
 *      path: envPath });` — dotenv reads and populates `process.env`
 *      (non-overriding by default) and the function immediately `return`s.
 *      Using `require` *inside* the function (instead of at the top of the
 *      file) is what makes dotenv optional: the require itself is the
 *      availability test.
 *   3. **Fallback trigger:** the bare `catch { /* dotenv not installed —
 *      parse .env manually *\/ }` swallows *any* failure from step 2 —
 *      module-not-found, a corrupted dotenv install, or a config error —
 *      and falls through. The comment documents the *expected* cause.
 *   4. **Fallback path:** `if (fs.existsSync(envPath))` guards
 *      `parseEnvText(fs.readFileSync(envPath, "utf8"));`. `existsSync` keeps
 *      a missing `.env` completely silent (a legal state: real env vars may
 *      be provided by the platform); only an actual read/parse failure
 *      reaches step 5.
 *   5. **Last-resort logging:** the outer `catch (err)` around the fallback
 *      prints `console.warn("Could not read .env:", err.message)` and
 *      returns. The server still boots; `PORT` falls back to `3000` in
 *      `server.js`, and a missing `FILES_ROOT` surfaces later as the
 *      structured `503 FILES_ROOT_MISSING` error from
 *      `lib/files-service.js`'s `resolveConfiguredRoot()` — with a message
 *      that even tells the operator to "Add it to your .env file".
 *   6. **Return value:** `undefined` on every path. All communication is via
 *      `process.env` and (worst case) the warn line. This is what lets
 *      `server.js` call it bare at top level with no error handling.
 * - **📥 Inputs:**
 *     - `projectRoot` — absolute path to the repository root (in this
 *       project, always `server.js`'s `PROJECT_ROOT = __dirname`).
 *     - File on disk: `.env` at that root (template: `.env.example`).
 *     - `node_modules/dotenv` — optional; declared in `package.json`.
 * - **📤 Outputs:** Populated `process.env` (`PORT`, `FILES_ROOT`, plus any
 *   other keys present in `.env`); at most one `console.warn` line; no
 *   exceptions, ever.
 * - **🔗 Project Dependencies:**
 *     - Depends on: `fs`, `path` (module scope), `dotenv` (optional),
 *       `parseEnvText` (fallback), the `.env` file.
 *     - Consumed by: **exactly one caller** — `server.js`:
 *       `loadEnv(PROJECT_ROOT)` at module top level, immediately after
 *       computing `PROJECT_ROOT` and immediately before
 *       `require("./lib/files-service")`.
 *     - @see server.js — the only consumer, and the owner of the call order.
 *     - @see lib/files-service.js — lazy consumer of the written
 *       `FILES_ROOT`.
 *     - @see package.json — declares `"dotenv": "^16.4.7"` and the
 *       `"start": "node server.js"` script that triggers this whole chain.
 *     - @see nginx.conf — its `/api/` comment states "FILES_ROOT is
 *       configured in the Node process .env — not in this file", i.e. this
 *       module is the single source of truth for that setting.
 * - **⚠️ Breaking Change Warning:**
 *   - **Call order is a contract.** `server.js` requires `lib/files-service.js`
 *     *after* calling `loadEnv`. That is safe today because files-service
 *     reads `FILES_ROOT` lazily — but if it ever hoists
 *     `process.env.FILES_ROOT` to module top level, any reordering breaks
 *     every request with `FILES_ROOT_MISSING`. Do not "optimise" the boot
 *     sequence without reading `dox/main/server.dox`'s Environment Bootstrap
 *     section, which documents the same contract from the caller's side.
 *   - **Never-throw is a contract.** `server.js` calls `loadEnv` with no
 *     surrounding `try`. Letting any error escape here turns a bad `.env`
 *     into a full boot crash instead of a degraded start.
 *   - Changing `path.join(projectRoot, ".env")` (filename, or the meaning of
 *     `projectRoot`) means the file silently stops being found — no error,
 *     just default port 3000 and eventual `FILES_ROOT_MISSING` responses.
 *   - Making dotenv a hard top-level require removes the resilience the
 *     header comment promises and breaks boots where `node_modules` is
 *     incomplete.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/load-env.js le_snip_loadenv
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @subsection sub_le_exports Block: module.exports — The Public Surface
 *
 * - **🎯 Purpose:** Publishes the module's single public function,
 *   `loadEnv`, using Node's CommonJS named-export object form.
 * - **🧒 ELI5:** The reader hangs one name tag on the door: "Ask for
 *   loadEnv." Everything else (the parser, the tools) stays in the back
 *   room where nobody can bother it.
 * - **⚙️ How It Works (Step-by-Step):**
 *   1. `module.exports = { loadEnv };` uses ES2015 shorthand property
 *      syntax — equivalent to `{ loadEnv: loadEnv }`.
 *   2. Replacing (not merging into) `module.exports` means the module's
 *      public surface is exactly one key; `parseEnvText` remains private by
 *      construction, so no other file can accidentally couple to the
 *      fallback parser's behaviour.
 *   3. Consumers must destructure: `const { loadEnv } = require("./lib/load-env")`
 *      — which is precisely how `server.js` does it, and how the require
 *      would appear in any future test file.
 * - **📥 Inputs:** The module-scope `loadEnv` function binding.
 * - **📤 Outputs:** The module's export object; after this line, the module
 *   is cacheable by Node's require registry and `server.js`'s destructure
 *   succeeds.
 * - **🔗 Project Dependencies:**
 *     - Depends on: `loadEnv` (defined above in the same file).
 *     - Consumed by: `server.js` — `const { loadEnv } = require("./lib/load-env");`.
 *     - @see server.js
 *     - @see lib/files-service.js — sibling `lib/` module using the same
 *       CommonJS export style for its (larger) service surface.
 * - **⚠️ Breaking Change Warning:** Renaming the key (`{ load: loadEnv }`)
 *   or switching to `exports.loadEnv = loadEnv` (merged-exports style)
 *   compiles fine but makes `server.js`'s destructured `loadEnv`
 *   `undefined` → `TypeError: loadEnv is not a function` at the very first
 *   line of boot — the whole server dies instantly. Keep the key name
 *   identical to the function name, and keep the file's public surface at
 *   exactly one export.
 *
 * @par 💻 Live Source Code:
 * @snippet lib/load-env.js le_snip_exports
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * @section le_contracts 📐 Conventions & Contracts
 *
 * Everything this file defines, produces or promises, and who relies on it.
 *
 * | Symbol / Attribute | Type | Defined Here | Consumed By | Purpose |
 * |--------------------|------|--------------|-------------|---------|
 * | `parseEnvText(text)` | `function(string): undefined` | Yes (private, not exported) | `loadEnv` fallback path only | Minimal KEY=VALUE → `process.env` parser with comment/blank-line skipping, quote stripping, non-override writes |
 * | `loadEnv(projectRoot)` | `function(string): undefined` | Yes (sole export) | `server.js` (module top level) | One-shot synchronous `.env` bootstrap; prefers dotenv, falls back to `parseEnvText`, never throws |
 * | `module.exports = { loadEnv }` | `object` | Yes | `server.js` via destructuring require | The module's entire public surface |
 * | `fs`, `path` | Node built-in bindings | Yes (module scope) | `loadEnv` only | `existsSync`/`readFileSync` and OS-portable `path.join(projectRoot, ".env")` |
 * | `process.env.PORT` | `string \| undefined` | No — *written* here | `server.js`: `const PORT = process.env \|\| 3000` | HTTP listen port for the whole back end |
 * | `process.env.FILES_ROOT` | `string \| undefined` | No — *written* here | `lib/files-service.js`: `resolveConfiguredRoot()` (per request) | Absolute or project-relative path of the downloads tree |
 * | Never-override rule | Behavioural contract | Yes (both parser and dotenv defaults) | Docker/systemd/CI deployments that export real env vars | Real environment variables always beat `.env` values |
 * | Never-throw rule | Behavioural contract | Yes (`try`/`catch` layers) | `server.js` top level (no error handling around the call) | A broken `.env` degrades boot, never crashes it |
 * | `.env` file | Filesystem input | No — read here | This module only (server-side) | Deployment configuration; template in `.env.example`; network-protected by `server.js`'s `/.env` blocklist and nginx deny rules |
 * | `dotenv` package | Optional dependency | No — `package.json` (`^16.4.7`) | `loadEnv` preferred path | Full-featured `.env` parser; absence triggers the built-in fallback |
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
